- Relationship with husband: Poor or bad relationship quality is likely a strong risk factor for postpartum depression; good relationships act as protective factors.
- Addiction: Addiction (smoking, drinking, drugs) may increase risk due to associated stress and health factors.
- Number of the latest pregnancy: First-time mothers or higher parity may have different risks; first pregnancy can be more stressful due to inexperience.
- Relationship between father and newborn: Poor or neutral relationship may increase maternal stress and risk; very good or good relationships are protective.
- Age of newborn: Early postpartum period (0 to 6 months) is the highest risk time for postpartum depression.
- Number of household members: Larger household may mean more support or more stress; smaller household may mean less support and more isolation.

